SELECT c.title, e.student_id, e.score
FROM enrollments e
JOIN courses c ON c.id = e.course_id
WHERE e.score = (
  SELECT MAX(x.score) FROM enrollments x WHERE x.course_id = e.course_id
);

approaches.ideaEn:
The masked address is three pieces glued together:

1. the first character, `SUBSTR(email, 1, 1)`;
2. the literal `'***'`;
3. everything from `@` to the end, `SUBSTR(email, INSTR(email, '@'))`, which keeps the @ and the domain.

SQLite concatenates with `||`. The middle is always exactly three stars whatever its length, so no length arithmetic is needed. Keep the original case; no LOWER.

approaches.sql:
SELECT id,
       SUBSTR(email, 1, 1) || '***' || SUBSTR(email, INSTR(email, '@')) AS masked
FROM customers;

approaches.ideaEn:
Rather than listing every way an address can be bad, write down what makes it good and negate the whole thing with `NOT`:

- exactly one `@`: `LENGTH(email) - LENGTH(REPLACE(email, '@', '')) = 1`, since removing every @ shortens the string by the number of @s;
- at least one character before it: `INSTR(email, '@') > 1`;
- the domain (computed once in a CTE) contains a dot: `domain LIKE '%.%'`;
- the domain neither starts nor ends with a dot: `NOT LIKE '.%'` and `NOT LIKE '%.'`.

Why not `LIKE '%_@_%._%'`? `%` matches any characters, @ included, so an address with two @s still matches, and so does a domain starting with a dot like `a@.b.com`. Separate rules leave no such gaps.

